[Chapter "Key ideas: Why the Slav"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{2...c6 guards d5, as 2...e6 does in the Queen's Gambit Declined, but it leaves the c8 bishop's diagonal open. The price: the b8 knight can't use c6, and once the bishop has left c8, nothing guards b7. The first board shows the bishop coming out before ...e6; the second shows the move order that keeps b7 safe in the main line.}
*

[Chapter "The bishop comes out first"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "4.e3"]

{The Quiet Variation: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3. In the Queen's Gambit Declined, ...e6 comes first, and the c8 bishop stays shut in behind its own pawns. In the Slav, the bishop comes out first, and ...e6 follows.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 {[%cal Gc8f5] [%csl Yc8]}
4... Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] The bishop is out, while White's c1 bishop is shut in behind e3. Don't take on c4 here: after 4...dxc4?! 5.Bxc4, White's bishop takes back and develops in one move (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).}
5. Nf3 e6 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6] The pawns on c6, d5 and e6 form a solid wall, and the bishop stands outside it. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Take on c4 first"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "4.Nf3"]

{Both White knights are out: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Nf3. If the bishop comes out now, cxd5 and Qb3 hit b7, which the bishop no longer guards. So Black takes on c4 first. With no pawn left on d5, there is no cxd5, and the bishop comes out next move.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 {[%cal Rd1b3,Rb3b7] [%csl Yb7]}
4... dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] Takes the pawn. Compare 4...Bf5?!: after 5.cxd5 cxd5 6.Qb3 the bishop has to go back with 6...Bc8, and after 7.Bg5 e6 8.e3 it is shut in after all (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).}
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Stops ...b5, which would hold the extra pawn.} Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] Now the bishop comes out: this is the Czech Variation. Trying to keep the pawn with 5...a6 and ...b5 only costs time (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).}
6. e3 {Opens the f1 bishop's path to c4.} e6 7. Bxc4 {The pawn is back.} Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Black's bishop stands outside the pawn chain, and the other bishop hits the knight on c3. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Plans for both sides"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{The plans repeat from game to game. The first board is Black's main line, the Czech Variation, where White aims for e4 and e5. The next two are from the Quiet Variation (the e3 lines), and the last is from the Exchange.}
*

[Chapter "Czech Variation: e4 and e5"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "9...Nbd7"]

{The Czech Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Nf3 dxc4 5.a4 Bf5 6.e3 e6 7.Bxc4 Bb4 8.O-O O-O 9.Qe2 Nbd7. White's plan is e4, which gains space and hits the bishop, then e5, which drives the knight from f6. Black keeps the bishop with ...Bg6 and ...Bh5, and the knight goes to d5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. a4 Bf5 6. e3 e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 Nbd7 {[%cal Re3e4,Re4e5,Gf5g6,Gg6h5]}
10. e4 {[%cal Ge4f5] Gains space and hits the bishop.} Bg6 {[%cal Gg6b1] Steps back, but stays on the same diagonal.}
11. Bd3 {Guards e4, so that e5 can follow.} Bh5 {[%cal Gh5e2] Pins the knight on f3 to the queen on e2.}
12. e5 {[%cal Ge5f6] Drives the knight away, but no White pawn can guard d5 any more.} Nd5 {[%csl Yd5] The knight takes the square.}
13. Nxd5 cxd5 {[%cal Gb4e7,Gh5g6] The center is locked, and White has more space on the kingside. Black's plan: ...Be7, then ...Bg6 to trade the light-squared bishops. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: Nh4 and ...hxg6"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "5...e6"]

{The Quiet Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3 Bf5 5.Nf3 e6. White's knight can chase the bishop from h4 and take it, to win the bishop pair. Keep the bishop on its diagonal with ...Bg6, and if the knight takes, take back with the h-pawn.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 {[%cal Gf3h4,Gh4f5]}
6. Nh4 {Hits the bishop.} Bg6 {Steps back and still watches e4 and b1.}
7. Nxg6 {White has the two bishops.} hxg6 {[%cal Gh8h1] Take back toward the center. The h-file opens for the rook on h8, one reason not to hurry with castling short. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: the ...c5 break"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "8...Be7"]

{The Quiet Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3 Bf5 5.Nf3 e6 6.Bd3 Bxd3 7.Qxd3 Nbd7 8.O-O Be7. The wall on c6, d5 and e6 is solid, but it leaves Black's pieces little room. When White takes the center with e4, Black trades on e4 and hits d4 with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. Nf3 e6 6. Bd3 Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Nbd7 8. O-O Be7 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6]}
9. e4 {White takes the center.} dxe4 10. Nxe4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 before White can support it.}
11. Be3 cxd4 12. Bxd4 O-O {Black has no weak pawns and a safe king. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Exchange Variation: the open c-file"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "8...Nd7"]

{The Exchange Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.cxd5 cxd5 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.Nf3 Nc6 6.Bf4 Bf5 7.e3 e6 8.Bb5 Nd7. The pawns are symmetrical and the c-file is open, so neither side has a weakness, and the side with the more active pieces is better. Both sides want a rook on the c-file. For Black, the rook also guards the knight on c6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Nf3 Nc6 6. Bf4 Bf5 7. e3 e6 8. Bb5 Nd7 {[%cal Gc8c1,Gc1c8]}
9. Qa4 {[%cal Gb5c6,Ga4c6] The bishop and the queen both hit the knight on c6.} Rc8 {Guards the knight and takes the open file. 9...Be7?! drops a pawn: 10.Bxc6 bxc6 11.Qxc6 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).}
10. O-O Be7 11. Rac1 {White's rook takes the c-file too.} O-O {Black's king is safe, and the knight on c6 is guarded. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: The c4-pawn and the c8 bishop"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Take on c4 when White's f1 bishop can't take back at once: after both knights are out, or after an early Bg5 or Bf4. If White hasn't played a4, Black can hold the pawn with ...b5, and when a4 comes, ...b4 drives the knight from c3. In these ...b5 lines, the c8 bishop goes to b7 instead of f5.}
*

[Chapter "Geller Gambit: 5...b5 and ...b4"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "5.e4"]

{The Geller Gambit: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Nf3 dxc4 5.e4. White builds a big center and leaves c4 alone. Black holds the pawn with ...b5. When White hits the chain with a4, ...b4 drives away the knight that guards e4.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. e4 {[%cal Gb7b5,Gb5c4]}
5... b5 {[%csl Yc4] Holds c4. After 5...Bg4 or 5...e6, White wins the pawn back and keeps the big center (Stockfish +1.0 and +1.5, clearly better for White).}
6. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits the base of the chain.} b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight, which guards e4.}
7. Na2 {The knight has to leave, and e4 loses its guard.} Nxe4 {Takes a second pawn.}
8. Bxc4 e6 {[%cal Gf8b4] White has won one pawn back, so Black is a pawn up, and White's pieces are more active. ...e6 opens the f8 bishop's diagonal, so 9.Nxb4? Bxb4+ wins the knight. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Alekhine Variation: the queenside majority"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "5.e3"]

{The Alekhine Variation: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Nf3 dxc4 5.e3. The same ...b5 and ...b4, but this time White wins the c4-pawn back. What Black keeps is a queenside majority: three pawns (a, b and c) against two. The c8 bishop goes to b7, on the long diagonal.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. e3 {[%cal Gb7b5,Gc8b7]}
5... b5 {[%cal Gb5c4] Holds c4 for now.} 6. a4 b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight.}
7. Na2 e6 {[%cal Gf8e7] Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal.}
8. Bxc4 {The pawn is back.} Bb7 {[%cal Gb7f3] [%csl Ya7,Yb4,Yc6] The bishop takes the long diagonal. Black has three pawns against two on the queenside. Next ...Nbd7, ...Be7, castle, and ...c5 to free the game. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Key ideas: Tricks and traps"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "ideas"]

{Four patterns to know. In the first two, White's queen on b3 hits b7 once the c8 bishop has left. In the last two, Black sets the trap. Each board starts just before the key move; the trap chapters show more of each line.}
*

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: 6.Qb3 b6?"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "6.Qb3"]

{The Quiet Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3 Bf5 5.cxd5 cxd5 6.Qb3. The queen hits b7 and d5. Go back with 6...Bc8!: Black loses time, but nothing else (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). The natural 6...b6? fails to a check.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 {[%cal Rb3b7,Rb3d5] [%csl Yb7]}
6... b6? {Guards b7, but opens the diagonal from b5 to Black's king.}
7. Bb5+! {Any piece that blocks on d7 cuts the queen on d8 off from d5.} Bd7
8. Nxd5! {Now only the knight on f6 guards d5.} Nxd5 9. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5a8,Gb5d7] White has won the d5-pawn and hits the rook on a8 and the bishop on d7. Stockfish: +2.4, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Exchange Variation: 7.Qb3 Na5!"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "7.Qb3"]

{The Exchange Variation after 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.cxd5 cxd5 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.Bf4 Nc6 6.e3 Bf5 7.Qb3. The same attack on b7, but here the knight on c6 can hit the queen. The natural 7...Qd7? leaves White clearly better (Stockfish +1.1), and 7...b6? is worse still (Stockfish +2.0, winning for White).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 Bf5 7. Qb3 {[%cal Rb3b7] [%csl Yb7]}
7... Na5! {[%cal Ga5b3] Hits the queen, so White has no time to take on b7.}
8. Qa4+ {The most common reply.} Bd7 {Blocks the check and hits the queen again.}
9. Qc2 e6 {The queen has gone back, and Black has gained time. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Early bishop: 6.a4? b4!"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "5...b5"]

{A trap you set: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.Nc3 Nf6 4.Bg5 dxc4 5.e4 b5. In the Geller Gambit, a4 and ...b4 are fine for White, but here White has a bishop on g5, not a knight on f3. After ...b4 the knight on c3 has to leave, and then ...Nxe4 takes a pawn and hits the bishop on g5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxc4 5. e4 b5 {[%cal Ga2a4,Gb5b4]}
6. a4? {Hits b5, but the knight on c3 has no good square once ...b4 comes.} b4! {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight, which guards e4. Against 7.Bxf6, take the knight on c3 first: 7...bxc3! (Stockfish −5.0, winning for Black).}
7. Na2?! {The most common reply. 7.Nb1 is better, but after 7...Nxe4 Black is two pawns up (Stockfish −2.3, winning for Black).} Nxe4! {[%cal Ge4g5] Takes a second pawn and hits the bishop on g5.}
8. Be3 {Saves the bishop.} b3! {[%cal Gb3a2] Hits the knight on a2, which has no good square. Stockfish: −3.5, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Exchange Variation: 7.e3? Qa5+!"]
[Type "concept"]
[Diagram "6...dxe4"]

{A trap you set: 1.d4 d5 2.c4 c6 3.cxd5 cxd5 4.Nc3 Nf6 5.Bg5 Ne4 6.Nxe4 dxe4. Black's knight hit the bishop and the knight on c3, and after the trade nothing guards the bishop on g5. A queen check from a5 would hit the bishop too.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bg5 Ne4 6. Nxe4 dxe4 {[%cal Gd8a5,Ga5g5] [%csl Yg5]}
7. e3? {Natural, but the pawn on e3 cuts the bishop off from d2, so the bishop can no longer block a check on the a5–e1 diagonal.} Qa5+! {[%cal Ga5e1,Ga5g5] Check, and the queen also hits the bishop on g5.}
8. Qd2 {Blocks the check, but the bishop on g5 still falls.} Qxg5 {Black has won a bishop for nothing. Stockfish: −6.1, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav: 4...e6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Exchange Variation, Reshevsky Variation"]
[ECO "D36"]

{The Slav (2...c6) comes up in a quarter of your Queen's Gambit games. Play 3.Nf3 and 4.Nc3. Against 4...e6, the most common reply, take on d5. After 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bg5 the pawns are the same as in the Exchange Variation, with Black's pawn already on c6 and White's knight on f3. The plans are the same too: Bd3, Qc2, castle, and the minority attack.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 {The Slav. The c-pawn guards d5 and keeps the c8 bishop's diagonal open.}
3. Nf3 {Develops and guards d4.} Nf6 4. Nc3 {This is the Three Knights Variation.} e6 {The Semi-Slav. Black shuts in the c8 bishop again, but d5 is very solid.}
5. cxd5 {[%cal Gc4d5] Fixes the pawns and steers the game into a structure you already know.

5...cxd5, about three games in ten, is under Advanced variations. 5...Nxd5 gives White the center after 6.e4 (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White).} exd5
6. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6] The same pin as in the Exchange Variation. Against 6...h6, keep the pin with 7.Bh4. After 7...Bf5 8.e3, develop with Bd3 and trade bishops (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} Be7
7. e3 {7...Bf5 can be met by 8.Nh4, which chases the bishop and gets ready to take it (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 7...Bg4, play 8.Qc2 and Bd3 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} O-O 8. Bd3 Nbd7
9. Qc2 {This is the Reshevsky Variation, reached by another move order.} Re8 10. O-O Nf8
11. Rab1 {[%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5] The same minority attack as in the Exchange: b4, b5 and bxc6. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Early bishop: 4.Bg5 and 4.Bf4"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "4.Bg5"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D10"]

{After 3.Nc3 Nf6, about one White player in five plays 4.Bg5, and one in ten plays 4.Bf4. White hasn't played e3 yet, so the f1 bishop can't take back on c4 at once. Take the pawn, and hold it with ...b5 when White plays e4 or e3. Stockfish rates 4...dxc4 as equal in both cases. After White's most common reply, 4.Bg5 dxc4 5.e4 b5, it prefers Black.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5
(4. Bf4 dxc4 {The same idea (Stockfish −0.2, equal).}
5. e3 {The most common move. Against 5.e4, play 5...b5 (Stockfish −0.5, slightly better for Black). Against 5.a4, play 5...Nd5, which hits the bishop on f4 and the knight on c3 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). After 5.e4 b5 6.a4 b4, 7.e5? runs into 7...bxc3!, as in the trap chapter on 6.a4? b4!.} b5
6. a4 {Two White players in three hit b5.} Qb6 {[%cal Gb6b5] The queen guards b5, so after axb5 Black takes back with the c-pawn and keeps the chain.}
7. axb5?! {The most common move, but now Black keeps a solid extra pawn. 7.Nf3, about one game in five, is better: 7...e6 and ...Bb4 (Stockfish −0.5, slightly better for Black).} cxb5 {[%csl Yb5,Yc4] The pawn on b5 guards c4.}
8. Nf3 {Against 8.b3, which hits c4, play 8...e6 9.bxc4 Bb4 (Stockfish −1.8, clearly better for Black).} e6 {[%cal Gf8b4] Next ...Bb4 and castle. Black is a solid pawn up. Stockfish: −1.9, clearly better for Black.})
4... dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] Take the pawn. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.}
5. e4 {The most common move, about one game in three.

Against 5.e3, play 5...b5, and after 6.a4, 6...Qb6 keeps the chain (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 5.a4, play 5...Qb6, which hits b2 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). If then 6.Bxf6?, take the pawn with 6...Qxb2!: White has to trade queens and ends up two pawns down (Stockfish −3.3, winning for Black). Against 5.Bxf6, take back with 5...exf6: the bishop on f8 and the queen get open lines (Stockfish −0.8, slightly better for Black).} b5 {[%cal Gb5c4] Holds c4. Stockfish: −0.5, slightly better for Black.}
6. e5 {[%cal Re5f6] Chases the knight. 6.a4? is a mistake: see the trap chapter on 6.a4? b4!.

Against 6.Nf3, play 6...h6, asking the bishop to decide (Stockfish −0.5, slightly better for Black). Against 6.a3, play 6...e6 (Stockfish −0.8, slightly better for Black). Against 6.Bxf6, take back with 6...exf6 and keep the extra pawn (Stockfish −1.4, clearly better for Black).} Nd5 {[%csl Yd5] The knight takes the central square.}
7. a4 {Against 7.Nf3, take the knight: 7...Nxc3 8.bxc3, and White's pawns are weak (Stockfish −1.3, clearly better for Black). Against 7.Nxd5, take back with 7...cxd5: Black is a solid pawn up (Stockfish −2.1, winning for Black). Against 7.Ne4, 7...Qb6 hits d4 and b2 (Stockfish −1.6, clearly better for Black).} h6 {[%cal Gh6g5] Asks the bishop to decide before Black plays ...b4.}
8. Bh4 {Keeps the bishop on the d8–h4 diagonal. Against 8.Bd2, play 8...a5 (Stockfish −0.7, slightly better for Black).} b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight.}
9. Ne4 g5 {[%cal Gg5h4] Drives the bishop off the diagonal.}
10. Bg3 Bf5 {[%cal Gf5e4] Hits the knight on e4. Black is a pawn up with active pieces. Stockfish: −1.1, clearly better for Black.} *

[Chapter "Exchange: 3.cxd5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Exchange Variation, Symmetrical Line"]
[ECO "D14"]

{About one game in five, White trades on d5: 3.cxd5 at once, or after 3.Nc3 Nf6 or 3.Nf3 Nf6. The pawns are symmetrical, so White, who moves first, keeps a small edge, but there is little risk for Black. Copy White's setup: ...Nf6, ...Nc6, ...Bf5 and ...e6. Watch for Qb3 hitting b7, and meet Bg5 with ...Ne4: both are in the trap chapters.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 {This is the Exchange Variation.} cxd5
4. Nc3 {Two White players in three. 4.Nf3 Nf6 5.Nc3 Nc6 reaches the position after 5...Nc6 below. Against 4.Bf4, play 4...Nc6, then ...Nf6, ...Bf5 and ...e6 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} Nf6
5. Nf3 {About one White player in three.

Against 5.Bf4, play 5...Nc6 6.e3 Bf5: after 7.Nf3 e6 the game is back in this chapter, and 7.Qb3 is the trap chapter "Qb3 in the Exchange". Against 7.Bd3, trade bishops: 7...Bxd3 8.Qxd3 e6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 5.Bg5, play 5...Ne4!: see the trap chapter on ...Qa5+.} Nc6
6. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The most common move, four games in ten. Against 6.Bg5, about one game in four, play 6...Ne4!, which hits the bishop and the knight on c3 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 6.e3, 6...e6 and ...Bd6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.g3, play 6...Bf5 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Bf5 {This is the Symmetrical Line: Black copies White's moves.}
7. e3 {Against 7.Qb3, play 7...Na5!, as in the trap chapter on Qb3 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 7.a3, play 7...e6 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).} e6
8. Bb5 {[%cal Gb5c6,Gf3e5] Pins the knight, and Ne5 may follow. The most common move, three games in ten.

Against 8.Bd3, trade bishops: 8...Bxd3 9.Qxd3 Bb4 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 8.Qb3, play 8...Bb4 (Stockfish 0.0, equal). This is the Trifunovic Variation.} Nd7 {[%cal Gd7b6,Gd7e5] Covers e5, so the knight on f3 can't jump in, and can go to b6.}
9. O-O {Against 9.Qa4, which piles up on the pinned knight, guard it with 9...Rc8 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). 9...Be7?! drops a pawn to 10.Bxc6 bxc6 11.Qxc6 (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).} Be7 {Castle next. Stockfish: +0.1, equal. If White plays 10.Qa4, guard the pinned knight with 10...Rc8 first (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} *

[Chapter "Czech Variation: 5.a4 Bf5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech Variation, Classical System, Main Line"]
[ECO "D19"]

{Black's main line in this course. With both White knights out, take on c4 first. After 5.a4, which stops ...b5, don't try to keep the pawn: develop with ...Bf5, ...e6, ...Bb4 and castle while White wins it back. White then aims for e4. Meet it with ...Bg6 and ...Bh5, and meet e5 with ...Nd5. Against the Krause Attack, 6.Ne5, see the branch.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 {3.Nf3 Nf6 4.Nc3 reaches the same position. Against White's other fourth moves, see the other chapters.} dxc4 {[%csl Yc4] Take the pawn first. There is no pawn left on d5 for cxd5 and Qb3, and White needs time to win c4 back.}
(4... Bf5?! {Brings the bishop out, but now nothing guards b7.} 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and d5.} Bc8 {Black's best: the bishop goes back to guard b7.} 7. Bg5 e6 8. e3 {The bishop is shut in after all, and White has gained time. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.})
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] This is the Alapin Variation. It stops ...b5, so Black can't hold the extra pawn. About half of White players choose it. Against 5.e4 and 5.e3, see Geller Gambit: 5.e4 and Alekhine Variation: 5.e3. Against the rare 5.Bg5, play 5...h6, and after 6.Bh4 keep the pawn with ...b5 (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black).} Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] This is the Czech Variation. The bishop comes out before ...e6 can shut it in.}
6. e3 {The most common move, six games in ten. It opens the f1 bishop's path to c4.

Against 6.Bg5, about one game in eight, play 6...Ne4!, which hits the bishop and the knight on c3 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). The natural 6...e6?! allows 7.e4!, which hits the bishop and threatens e5 (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). Against 6.Bf4, play 6...e6 and ...Bb4 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). Against 6.Nh4, which chases the bishop, both 6...e6 and 6...Bg6 keep the balance (Stockfish +0.2, equal).}
(6. Ne5 {This is the Krause Attack, Stockfish's favorite. The knight heads for c4 to win the pawn back, and f3 and e4 may follow to hit the bishop on f5. About one game in six.} Nbd7 {[%cal Gd7e5] Challenges the knight at once. The main alternative, 6...e6, leads to sharp lines with a piece sacrifice: 7.f3 Bb4 8.e4 Bxe4 9.fxe4 Nxe4 (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).}
7. Nxc4 {Almost every White player takes the pawn back.} Nb6 {[%cal Gb6c4,Gb6a4] Hits the knight on c4 and the pawn on a4.}
8. Ne5 {The most common move, two games in three. Against 8.e3, play 8...e6 and ...Bb4 (Stockfish +0.3). Against 8.Ne3, play 8...e6, and if 9.Nxf5, 9...exf5: the doubled pawns control e4 (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} a5 {[%csl Ya5] Stops a5, which would chase the knight from b6. Next ...e6, ...Bb4 and castle. White has a little more space, but Black is solid. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.})
6... e6 7. Bxc4 Bb4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Pins the knight on c3, so e4 is harder to play. Against 8.Qb3 or 8.Bd2, play 8...a5, which keeps the bishop on b4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).}
8. O-O {This is the Dutch Variation.} O-O
9. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4,Gf1d1] Prepares e4 and Rd1. The most common move, one game in four. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.

Against 9.Qb3, play 9...a5 (Stockfish +0.2, equal). Against 9.Nh4, play 9...Nbd7: if White takes on f5, the pawn on f5 controls e4 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 9.Bd2, 9...c5 hits the center at once (Stockfish 0.0, equal). Against 9.h3 or 9.Ne5, 9...Nbd7 (Stockfish +0.2 and +0.1, equal).} Nbd7 {Develops the last minor piece and gets ready for e4.}
10. e4 {Two White players in three play it. Against 10.Rd1, 10...Ne4 hits c3 and trades pieces (Stockfish +0.2, equal).} Bg6 {[%cal Gg6b1] Steps back but keeps the bishop on the b1–h7 diagonal.}
11. Bd3 {[%cal Re4e5] Guards e4 and gets ready for e5. Against 11.e5, 11...Nd5 takes the square at once (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). Against 11.Bg5, 11...h6 asks the bishop to decide (Stockfish −0.2, equal).} Bh5 {[%cal Gh5f3] Pins the knight on f3 to the queen on e2.}
12. e5 {Drives the knight away, but gives up the d5 square.} Nd5 {[%csl Yd5] The knight takes the square White's pawn left behind.}
13. Nxd5 cxd5 {[%cal Gb4e7,Gh5g6,Ga8c8] The center is locked, and White has more space on the kingside. Black plans ...Be7, ...Bg6 to trade the light-squared bishops, and a rook to c8. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav: 3...e6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense: Noteboom Variation"]
[ECO "D31"]

{About one Slav player in seven plays 3...e6 before ...Nf6. Answer with 4.Nc3, as in the rest of the Slav. The most common reply, 4...Nf6, brings back Semi-Slav: 4...e6. This chapter shows the three replies that don't: the Noteboom Variation (4...dxc4), the Stonewall setup (4...f5) and 4...Bd6.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 e6 {Black holds d5 and keeps the knight on g8 for now, so ...dxc4 and ...f5 are both possible.}
4. Nc3 {Develops and adds a second attacker on d5.

4...Nf6 5.cxd5 exd5 6.Bg5 is Semi-Slav: 4...e6, reached by another move order. Against 4...Bb4, play 5.Qb3: after 5...Bxc3+ 6.Qxc3, White has the two bishops (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).} dxc4 {This is the Noteboom Variation. Black takes on c4 and wants to keep the pawn with ...b5 and ...Bb4.}
(4... f5?! {Black builds a Stonewall: pawns on c6, d5, e6 and f5. They hold e4, but no pawn can ever guard e5, and the c8 bishop is shut in.} 5. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The bishop comes out before ...Bd6 can offer a trade. 5...Bd6 6.e3 Nf6 reaches the same position as this line.} Nf6 6. e3 Bd6 {6...Be7 7.Bd3 O-O 8.O-O (Stockfish +1.1, clearly better for White). 6...Bb4 7.Bd3 (Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White).}
7. Bd3 {Against 7...Bxf4 8.exf4, White's knight gets the e5 square (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White).} O-O 8. O-O Ne4 {[%csl Ye4] The knight takes the square the Stonewall holds.}
9. Rc1 {[%cal Gc4c5] Next Qc2 or Qb3, and c5 to gain space on the queenside. Stockfish: +1.3, clearly better for White.})
(4... Bd6?! {The bishop doesn't stop e4, and it can be hit there.} 5. e4! {Takes the center with gain of time. Against 5...Ne7, play 6.Bd3 (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White).} dxe4 6. Nxe4 {Hits the bishop.

6...Nf6 7.Nxf6+ Qxf6 8.c5 Bc7 9.Bd3 (Stockfish +1.3, clearly better for White). 6...Bb4+ 7.Bd2 Bxd2+ 8.Qxd2 (Stockfish +1.6, clearly better for White). 6...Be7 7.Bd3 (Stockfish +1.6, clearly better for White).} Bc7 7. Bd3 {White has the center and quicker development. Castle next. Stockfish: +1.9, clearly better for White.})
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Stops ...b5 for now.

5...Nf6 6.e3 c5 7.Bxc4 reaches the position from the note at 5.a4 in Czech Variation: 4...dxc4 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White). Against 5...Bd7, take the center with 6.e4 (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} Bb4 {[%cal Rb4c3] Pins the knight, so that ...b5 can follow.}
6. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop: Bxc4 is the threat.

6...Bxc3+?! 7.bxc3 gives White the two bishops and a strong center for the pawn (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).} b5 {Holds c4.}
7. Bd2 {Unpins the knight, so it can move again.

7...a5 is the Abrahams Variation: 8.axb5 Bxc3 9.Bxc3 cxb5 10.b3 Bb7 11.bxc4 b4 12.Bb2. Black gets two connected passed pawns on the queenside, and White gets the center and the two bishops (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).} Bxc3 {Half of club players trade at once.}
8. Bxc3 Nf6 {8...Bb7?! runs into 9.d5!, which opens the long diagonal for White's bishop on c3 (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White).}
9. axb5 cxb5 10. b3 {[%cal Gb3c4] Hits the head of the chain, as in 3...dxc4. White wins the pawn back next. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.

After 10...O-O 11.bxc4 bxc4 12.Bxc4, White has the pawn back and the two bishops (Stockfish +0.3). Against 10...Nd5, play 11.Ba5!: it hits the queen and keeps the bishop away from the knight (Stockfish +0.6).} *

[Chapter "Semi-Slav: 5...cxd5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Semi-Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D43"]

{About three Semi-Slav players in ten take back on d5 with the c-pawn. The pawns are symmetrical, but Black's c8 bishop is shut in behind e6 and White's bishops are free. Develop with Bg5, e3 and Bd3, and use the open c-file.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 e6 5. cxd5 cxd5
6. Bg5 {[%cal Gg5f6] Pins the knight.} Be7 7. e3 O-O 8. Bd3 Nc6
9. Rc1 {[%cal Gc1c8] The rook takes the open c-file. Castle next. Stockfish: +0.3, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "4...Bf5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{About one Slav player in five develops the bishop at once with 4...Bf5. Black frees the bishop before playing ...e6, but b7 loses its guard. Take on d5 and play 6.Qb3: the queen hits b7, and d5 needs watching too. Many club players go wrong here (see the trap chapter on ...b6? and ...Qd7?). Against the best defenses, White keeps a small, lasting edge.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 {This is the Three Knights Variation.} Bf5 {[%csl Yb7] The bishop comes out before ...e6, but nothing guards b7 now.}
5. cxd5 {Opens the c-file, and the queen can reach b3.

About one player in four takes back with the knight. 6.Qb3 hits b7 again, and after 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 Qb6 8.Ne5, White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.6).} cxd5
6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and adds pressure on d5.

Black's best is 6...Bc8, which goes back to guard b7. Develop with 7.Bg5 e6 8.e3: Black has lost time, and the c8 bishop is shut in again (Stockfish +0.5, slightly better for White).

6...e6? lets White take a pawn: 7.Qxb7 Nbd7 8.Bf4 (Stockfish +1.5, clearly better for White). 6...b6? and 6...Qd7? run into 7.e4!: see the trap chapter.} Qb6 {Offers a queen trade to cover b7.}
7. Qxb6 {[%engine ok] Trades queens and leaves Black with doubled pawns. 7.Nxd5 is sharper: after 7...Nxd5 8.Qxd5 Qb4+ 9.Bd2 Qxb2 10.Rc1, Black wins the pawn back, and Stockfish rates both lines slightly better for White.} axb6 {[%csl Yb6,Yb7] Black's b-pawns are doubled, but the a-file is open for the rook.}
8. e3 {[%cal Gf3e5,Gc3b5] Next Ne5, Bd2 and Nb5, aimed at c7 and the doubled pawns. White has an easy endgame plan. Stockfish: +0.5, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "3...Bf5"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Modern Line"]
[ECO "D11"]

{About one Slav player in eight brings the bishop out on move 3. Now only the queen guards d5, and nothing guards b7. Take on d5 and play Qb3, which hits both. Every Black reply leaves White better, and the most common ones lose material.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Bf5
4. cxd5 {4...Qxd5 brings the queen out early: 5.Nc3 hits it (Stockfish +2.0, winning for White).} cxd5
5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and d5.

5...b6? and 5...Qd7? lose material, as in the trap chapter on ...b6? and ...Qd7?: 5...b6 6.e4! (Stockfish +4.1), or 5...Qd7 6.Ne5 Qc8 7.Nc3 (Stockfish +3.8). 5...Qb6 6.Qxd5 e6 7.Qb3 Qxb3 8.axb3 leaves White a pawn up in the endgame (Stockfish +1.3). Black's best is 5...Nf6, which gives up b7 for development: 6.Qxb7 Nbd7 7.Nc3 (Stockfish +0.9, slightly better for White).} Qc7? {Guards b7, but not d5.}
6. Nc3 {[%cal Gc3d5,Gc3b5] Hits d5 again, and Nb5 is coming.} Nf6 7. e4! {Opens lines while Black's king is in the center.} dxe4 8. Ne5 e6
9. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4c7] Hits the queen. Bb5+ comes next. Stockfish: +2.3, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "...Bg4 and Ne5"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "4...Bg4"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{Against an early ...Bg4, jump into e5 with the knight, which hits the bishop. Most Black players then go to h5 or f5, and both moves lose ground to cxd5 and Qb3, because b7 has no guard. Black's best is to go back to c8, with a slightly worse game.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6
(3... Bg4 4. Ne5 {Hits the bishop. 4...Bc8 is Black's best (Stockfish +0.8). After 4...Bf5?, 5.cxd5 Bxb1 6.dxc6 Nxc6 7.Nxc6 bxc6 8.Rxb1 leaves Black's pawns weak (Stockfish +2.6).} Bh5? {The most common reply, but the bishop leaves the queenside.} 5. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] b7 has no guard, and the bishop on h5 can't help.} Qc8 6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Nc3 {Black is behind in development, and d5 is under fire. Stockfish: +3.4, winning for White.})
4. Nc3 Bg4 5. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5g4] Hits the bishop.

Black's best is 5...Bc8 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White). After 5...Bf5?, 6.cxd5 and e4 gain time (Stockfish +2.6, winning for White). After 5...e6, 6.f3 chases the bishop with g4 and h4 (Stockfish +3.0, winning for White).} Bh5? {The most common reply, but the bishop leaves the queenside.}
6. cxd5 cxd5 7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and adds pressure on d5. Stockfish: +3.3, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Czech Variation: 4...dxc4"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Czech Variation, Classical System, Main Line"]
[ECO "D19"]

{About one Slav player in nine takes on c4 after 4.Nc3. Play 5.a4 to stop ...b5, which would hold the pawn. White wins the pawn back with e3 and Bxc4, castles, and prepares e4 with Qe2.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 dxc4
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] This is the Alapin Variation. It stops ...b5. Against 5...e6, play 6.e3 c5 7.Bxc4 (Stockfish +0.3, slightly better for White).} Bf5 {This is the Czech Variation. Black develops the bishop before ...e6.}
6. e3 {Opens the diagonal of the f1 bishop.} e6 7. Bxc4 {The pawn is back.} Bb4 {[%cal Rb4c3] Pins the knight, so e4 is harder to play.}
8. O-O {This is the Dutch Variation.} O-O 9. Qe2 {[%cal Ge3e4,Gf1d1] Prepares e4 and Rd1. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Geller Gambit: 5.e4"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Geller Gambit"]
[ECO "D15"]

{About three White players in ten answer 4...dxc4 with 5.e4, the Geller Gambit. White builds a big center and gives up the c4-pawn. Only one reply works: 5...b5!, which keeps it. After 6.a4 b4 the knight has to leave c3, and Black takes on e4 as well. After 6.e5 Nd5, Black's knight is strong and the extra pawn on the queenside is a long-term plus.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. e4 {This is the Geller Gambit.} b5! {[%cal Gb5c4] Holds the pawn. The natural 5...Bg4 or 5...e6 lets White win it back with a big center (Stockfish +1.0 and +1.4, clearly better for White).}
6. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits the chain at its base. About one White player in three.

Against 6.a3, play 6...e6 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black). Against 6.Bg5, 6...h6 asks the bishop where it is going (Stockfish −0.6, slightly better for Black). Against 6.Be2, 6...e6 and ...a5 (Stockfish 0.0, equal).}
(6. e5 {About one White player in four.} Nd5 {[%csl Yd5] The knight takes the central square.}
7. a4 {About half of White players hit b5 here. Against 7.Be2, play 7...e6 (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black). Against 7.Ne4, 7...Bf5 hits the knight (Stockfish −1.3, clearly better for Black). Against 7.Nxd5, 7...cxd5 keeps a solid extra pawn (Stockfish −1.7, clearly better for Black).} e6 {[%cal Gf8b4] The pawn guards the knight on d5, and the f8 bishop can come out.}
8. axb5 Nxc3 9. bxc3 cxb5 {[%csl Yb5,Yc4] Black is a pawn up, and the pawns on b5 and c4 support each other.}
10. Ng5 {[%cal Gd1h5,Gd1g4] White eyes Black's king with Qh5 or Qg4. Against 10.Be2, the same 10...Bb7 (Stockfish −1.4, clearly better for Black).} Bb7 {[%cal Gb7g2] The bishop takes the long diagonal. Next ...Be7 and castle. Stockfish: −1.2, clearly better for Black.})
6... b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight, which also guards e4.}
7. Na2 {The most common move. Against 7.e5, take the knight: 7...bxc3! 8.exf6 cxb2 9.Bxb2 exf6, and Black is two pawns up for the moment (Stockfish −0.9, slightly better for Black). Against 7.Nb1, take the e4-pawn: 7...Nxe4 (Stockfish −0.3).} Nxe4 {Takes a second pawn. White wins one back next move.}
8. Bxc4 {Against 8.Nxb4, play 8...e6 and ...Qa5+ later (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} e6 {[%cal Gf8e7] Opens the bishop's diagonal, so Black can castle.}
9. O-O {Against 9.Nxb4, 9...Bxb4+ wins the knight, because ...e6 opened the bishop's diagonal (Stockfish −6.5, winning for Black).} a5 {[%cal Ga5b4] Guards the b4-pawn, so the knight on a2 has no target.}
10. Re1 Nf6 {The knight steps back from the e-file. Black is a pawn up, and White has more active pieces. Stockfish: −0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "Quiet Variation: 4.e3 Bf5"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Quiet Variation, Schallopp Defense"]
[ECO "D12"]

{When White plays e3 early, the c1 bishop is shut in, so Black has no reason to take on c4 first: play ...Bf5 at once, then ...e6, ...Nbd7 and ...Be7, and castle. This is the Quiet Variation. Counting all its move orders (3.Nc3 Nf6 4.e3, 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.e3 and 3.e3 Nf6), it comes up in about one game in six. Watch for Qb3 hitting b7: see the trap chapter on ...b6?.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 {Shuts in the c1 bishop. 3.Nf3 Nf6 4.e3 Bf5 5.Nc3 e6 and 3.e3 Nf6 4.Nc3 Bf5 5.Nf3 e6 reach the same position as this chapter after 5...e6.} Bf5 {[%cal Gf5b1] The bishop comes out before ...e6.}
5. Nf3 {The most common move, about half the time.

Against 5.cxd5, take back with the pawn, and meet 6.Qb3 with 6...Bc8!: see the trap chapter on ...b6?. Against 5.Bd3, trade bishops: 5...Bxd3 6.Qxd3 e6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 5.Qb3, 5...Qb6 offers a queen trade (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Never 5...b6?, which runs into 6.cxd5 (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).} e6 {[%csl Yc6,Yd5,Ye6] This is the Schallopp Defense of the Quiet Variation. The pawns on c6, d5 and e6 form a wall, and the bishop sits outside it.}
6. Bd3 {Offers to trade bishops. White's most common move, one game in five.

Against 6.Be2, develop with 6...Nbd7 or 6...h6, and answer Nh4 with ...Bg6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). Against 6.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 6...exd5 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 6.a3, develop with 6...Nbd7 and ...Be7 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 6.Qb3, play 6...Qb6, and if 7.c5, step back with 7...Qc7! (Stockfish −0.2, equal). 7...Qxb3?!, the most common reply, lets White expand with 8.axb3 and b4 (Stockfish +0.7, slightly better for White).}
(6. Nh4 {[%cal Gh4f5] Chases the bishop: White wants the bishop pair. About one game in ten.} Bg6 {Steps back and keeps the bishop on the diagonal.}
7. Nxg6 {Four White players in five take the bishop at once.} hxg6 {[%cal Gh8h1] Take back toward the center. The h-file opens for Black's rook, one reason not to hurry with castling short. Vladimir Kramnik had this position as Black in his 2006 world championship match against Veselin Topalov.}
8. Bd3 Nbd7 {Next ...Bd6 or ...Be7, and later ...dxc4 with ...c5 or ...e5. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.})
6... Bxd3 7. Qxd3 Nbd7
8. O-O {Against 8.cxd5, take back with the e-pawn: 8...exd5 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} Be7 {Castle next.}
9. e4 {White takes the center. Against quieter moves, such as 9.b3, castle (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} dxe4
10. Nxe4 c5 {[%cal Gc5d4] Hits d4 before White can support it. 10...Nxe4 11.Qxe4 O-O is also fine (Stockfish +0.2, equal).}
11. Be3 {Against 11.d5, take back with 11...exd5 (Stockfish −0.1, equal).} cxd4 12. Bxd4 O-O {Black has no weaknesses and a safe king. Next ...Qc7 and a rook to d8. Stockfish: +0.1, equal.} *

[Chapter "3...dxc4"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Modern Line"]
[ECO "D11"]

{About one Slav player in twenty takes on c4 at once, and two in three of them try to keep the pawn with 4...b5. Then 5.a4 hits the chain, and b3 breaks it, as in the Accepted.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 dxc4 4. e3 {Bxc4 is coming.} b5
5. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Hits the base of the chain.} e6 6. axb5 cxb5 7. b3 {[%cal Gb3c4] Hits the head of the chain.} Nf6 8. bxc4 bxc4
9. Bxc4 {White has the pawn back and the better center. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Schlechter: 4...g6"]
[Side "white"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Schlechter Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{4...g6 comes up in about one Slav game in thirteen, and 4...a6, the Chebanenko Variation, in about one in twenty. Against 4...g6, take on d5 and put the bishop on f4, the diagonal that Black's fianchetto leaves alone. Against 4...a6, play 5.a4, which stops ...b5, and develop normally (Stockfish +0.2, equal).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 g6 {This is the Schlechter Variation. Black prepares ...Bg7.}
5. cxd5 {Against 5...Nxd5, 6.e4 takes the full center (Stockfish +1.0, clearly better for White).} cxd5
6. Bf4 {[%cal Gf4b8] The bishop takes the open diagonal.} Nc6 7. e3 {Next Bd3 or Be2, castle, and Rc1. Stockfish: +0.4, slightly better for White.} *

[Chapter "Alekhine Variation: 5.e3"]
[Side "black"]
[Type "line"]
[Level "advanced"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Alekhine Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{About one White player in eight plays the quiet 5.e3, getting ready for Bxc4. Keep the pawn with 5...b5. After 6.a4 b4, the knight has to leave c3, and White has to work to win c4 back. Then develop with ...e6, ...Bb7 and ...Nbd7, and break with ...c5.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. e3 {This is the Alekhine Variation.} b5 {[%cal Gb5c4] Holds c4. 5...Bf5?! 6.Bxc4 gives White an easy game (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White).}
6. a4 {[%cal Ga4b5] Three White players in four hit b5 at once. Against 6.Ne5, 6...e6 keeps the pawn and opens the bishop's diagonal (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} b4 {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight.}
7. Na2 {The most common move. Against 7.Nb1, 7...Ba6 guards c4 (Stockfish −0.1, equal). Against 7.Ne2, 7...a5 guards b4 (Stockfish −0.3, slightly better for Black).} e6 {[%cal Gf8e7,Gf8d6] Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal.}
8. Bxc4 Bb7 {[%cal Gb7f3] The bishop takes the long diagonal.}
9. O-O {Against 9.Bd2, about four games in ten, the bishop and the knight both hit b4: guard it with 9...a5 (Stockfish −0.2, equal). The natural 9...Nbd7? drops the pawn to 10.Nxb4 (Stockfish +0.8, slightly better for White).} Nbd7 {Next ...Be7 or ...Bd6, castle, and ...c5 to free the game. If White's bishop comes to d2 later, guard b4 with ...a5. Stockfish: −0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...b6? and ...Qd7? against Qb3"]
[Side "white"]
[Diagram "6...b6"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Three Knights Variation"]
[ECO "D15"]

{In the Slav with an early ...Bf5, 6.Qb3 hits b7. Many players guard it with 6...b6 or 6...Qd7. Both lose to 7.e4!: the pawn opens the e-file and the diagonal from f1 to a6, and the knight jumps to e5 with gain of time. Black's king is stuck in the center.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. Nc3 Bf5 5. cxd5 cxd5 6. Qb3 b6? {Guards b7, but Black is behind in development and the king is still on e8.}
(6... Qd7? 7. e4! Nxe4 8. Ne5 {Hits the queen.} Qc8 9. g4 {[%cal Gg4f5] The bishop on f5 is trapped. White wins a piece for a pawn. Stockfish: +4.0, winning for White.})
7. e4! {Opens lines while Black's king is still in the center.} dxe4
8. Ne5 {[%cal Ge5f7,Gf1b5] The knight eyes f7 and d7, and Bb5+ comes next.} Be6 9. Bb5+ Nfd7
10. Qc2 {[%cal Gc2e4] Hits the pawn on e4, and the queen on e4 will also aim at the rook on a8. Black's king is stuck in the center. Stockfish: +5.2, winning for White.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 6.a4? b4! against 4.Bg5"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.a4"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D10"]

{A trap you set. After 4.Bg5 dxc4 5.e4 b5, about one White player in four hits the chain with 6.a4?. With a knight on f3 instead of a bishop on g5 (the Geller Gambit), that idea is fine for White, but here it fails: after 6...b4!, the knight on c3 has to move, and it was guarding e4. Then ...Nxe4 also hits the bishop on g5. Two Black players in three find 6...b4, but the follow-up is tricky: against 7.Bxf6, take the knight on c3 first. White's best try is 7.Nb1, but after 7...Nxe4 Black has two extra pawns (Stockfish −1.9, clearly better for Black).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 dxc4 5. e4 b5
6. a4? {Hits b5, but leaves the knight on c3 without a safe square.} b4! {[%cal Gb4c3] Hits the knight, which guards e4.}
7. Na2?! {The most common reply, about half the time.}
(7. Bxf6? {Takes the knight first, about a third of the time.} bxc3! {Takes the other knight before taking back on f6. 7...exf6?, played by four Black players in ten, gives most of the advantage away (Stockfish −0.6, slightly better for Black).} 8. Be5 {The most common reply: the bishop escapes. After 8.bxc3 exf6, Black is a piece up.} cxb2 {[%cal Gb2a1] Hits the rook on a1.} 9. Rb1 Qa5+ {[%cal Ga5e1,Gc4c3] Check. Next ...c3, and the pawns on b2 and c3 cost White more material. Stockfish: −6.6, winning for Black.})
(7. e5? {Hits the knight, but leaves the knight on c3 to its fate.} bxc3! 8. exf6? {Almost every White player takes back here, but it loses even more. 8.bxc3 Ne4 was the lesser evil (Stockfish −5.6, winning for Black).} cxb2! {[%cal Gb2a1,Gd8a5] The pawn on b2 threatens to take the rook on a1 and become a queen, and ...Qa5+ is coming too. White can't cover everything and loses more material. Stockfish: −7.1, winning for Black.})
7... Nxe4! {[%cal Ge4g5] Takes a second pawn and hits the bishop on g5.}
8. Be3 {The most common move. Against 8.Bf4, 8...b3 works the same way (Stockfish −4.2, winning for Black). Against 8.Bh4, 8...Qa5 adds pressure on the queenside (Stockfish −4.7, winning for Black).} b3! {[%cal Gb3a2] Hits the knight on a2, which has no good square.}
9. Nc3 Nxc3 10. bxc3 Qa5 {[%cal Ga5c3] Hits c3. Black is two pawns up, and more material falls. Stockfish: −3.6, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: 5.Bg5 Ne4 and ...Qa5+"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "7.e3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D10"]

{A trap you set. In the Exchange, meet 5.Bg5 with 5...Ne4!: the knight hits the bishop on g5 and the knight on c3. After 6.Nxe4 dxe4, nothing guards the bishop on g5. If White plays the natural 7.e3?, about half the time, 7...Qa5+! checks and takes the bishop. Against White's other moves, Black is fine too: after 7.Bd2, 7...Qxd4 wins a pawn (Stockfish −0.4, slightly better for Black).}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bg5 Ne4 {[%cal Ge4g5,Ge4c3] Hits the bishop and the knight. Against 6.Bf4, play 6...Nc6 (Stockfish −0.1, equal); against 6.Bh4, take on c3: 6...Nxc3 7.bxc3 (Stockfish −0.2, equal).}
6. Nxe4 {The most common move, three in four.} dxe4 {[%csl Yg5] Now the bishop on g5 has no defender.}
7. e3? {Natural, but the pawn on e3 cuts the bishop off from d2, so the bishop can no longer block a check on the a5–e1 diagonal.} Qa5+! {[%cal Ga5e1,Ga5g5] Check, and the queen also hits the bishop on g5.}
8. Qd2 {Almost every White player blocks with the queen. 8.b4 Qxg5 is no better (Stockfish −6.0, winning for Black).} Qxg5 {Black has won a bishop for nothing. Stockfish: −6.0, winning for Black.} *

[Chapter "Trap: Qb3 in the Exchange"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "7.Qb3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Exchange Variation"]
[ECO "D10"]

{A trap to avoid. In the Exchange with Bf4 and e3, a popular setup at club level, Qb3 hits b7 once your bishop is on f5. Hit the queen with 7...Na5!. The natural 7...Qd7 and 7...b6 leave White clearly better, and together they are almost four replies in ten.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. cxd5 cxd5 4. Nc3 Nf6 5. Bf4 Nc6 6. e3 Bf5
7. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7] Hits b7. About one White player in four plays it here.} Na5! {[%cal Ga5b3] Hits the queen, so White has no time to take on b7. 7...Qb6 is the other safe reply, but after 8.Qxb6 axb6 White is slightly better (Stockfish +0.4, slightly better for White).}
(7... Qd7? {Guards b7, but the queen on d7 gets in the way of Black's pieces, and Ne5 or Bb5 can hit it.} 8. Nf3 a6 9. Be2 e6 10. O-O {[%cal Gf3e5,Ga1c1] White is ahead in development, with Ne5 and the c-file to come. Stockfish: +1.0, clearly better for White.})
(7... b6? {Guards b7 with a pawn, but weakens c6.} 8. Bb5 {[%cal Gb5c6] Pins the knight on c6.} Bd7 9. Rc1 e6 10. Bxc6 Bxc6 11. Nb5 {[%cal Gb5c7] Threatens Nc7+, which would fork the king and the rook. Black has to take the knight, and after 11...Bxb5 12.Qxb5+ Qd7 13.Qxd7+ White has the better endgame. Stockfish: +1.7, clearly better for White.})
8. Qa4+ {The most common move, two in three. Against 8.Bb5+, block with 8...Bd7, and after 9.Qc2, 9.Qa4 or 9.Bxd7+, the same plan works: ...e6 and ...Rc8 (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} Bd7 {Blocks the check and hits the queen.}
9. Qc2 {Against 9.Bb5, play 9...e6 and trade bishops on d7 if White wants (Stockfish +0.1, equal).} e6 {[%cal Ga8c8,Gf8b4] The queen has gone back, and Black has gained time. Next ...Rc8, ...Bb4 or ...Be7, and the knight comes back to c6 or c4. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

[Chapter "Trap: ...b6? against Qb3"]
[Side "black"]
[Diagram "6.Qb3"]
[Type "trap"]
[Opening "Slav Defense"]
[ECO "D10"]

{A trap to avoid. With the bishop on f5, nothing guards b7. After cxd5 cxd5, Qb3 hits b7 and d5 together. Go back with ...Bc8!: it costs time, but nothing else. The natural 6...b6? looks solid, but 7.Bb5+! wins the d5-pawn, because the piece that blocks the check on d7 no longer guards d5. One Black player in five plays 6...b6 here.}
1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e3 Bf5 5. cxd5 cxd5
6. Qb3 {[%cal Gb3b7,Gb3d5] Hits b7 and d5. About one White player in three plays it here.} Bc8! {Guards b7 again. The bishop comes back out later, to d7 or g4.

6...Qd7, the most common reply, also holds, but White keeps an edge (Stockfish +0.6, slightly better for White). 6...Qb6? loses a pawn to 7.Nxd5! Nxd5 8.Qxd5 (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).}
(6... b6? {Guards b7 with a pawn, but opens the diagonal from b5 to Black's king.} 7. Bb5+! {Check. The piece that blocks on d7 no longer guards d5.} Bd7 {The most common block.}
(7... Nbd7 8. Nxd5 Nxd5 9. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5a8] White has won the d5-pawn, and the queen hits the rook on a8 and the knight on d7. Stockfish: +3.8, winning for White.})
8. Nxd5! Nxd5 9. Qxd5 {[%cal Gd5a8,Gb5d7] White has won the d5-pawn and hits the rook on a8 and the bishop on d7. After the trades on b5 and d7, White is a clean pawn up in the endgame. Stockfish: +2.5, winning for White.})
7. Nf3 {The most common move. Against 7.Bb5+, block with 7...Nc6 (Stockfish +0.1, equal). 7...Nbd7? cuts the queen off from d5 again, and 8.Nxd5 wins a pawn (Stockfish +1.4, clearly better for White).} e6 {[%cal Gf8d6] Opens the f8 bishop's diagonal.}
8. Bd3 Nc6 {Black has developed safely. Next ...Bd6 or ...Be7, castle, and ...Bd7. Stockfish: +0.2, equal.} *

